=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router8
diagnosis_text:
The OSPF adjacency between router7 and router8 over their shared link
(172.25.1.0/24, router7.eth5 ↔ router8.eth4) is broken by an OSPF area
mismatch introduced during the recent change on router8.

router7 announces subnet 172.25.1.0/24 into area 0 (confirmed:
"Internet Address 172.25.1.1/24 … Area 0.0.0.0"), while router8 announces
the same subnet into area 7 ("Internet Address 172.25.1.2/24 … Area
0.0.0.7"). Both interfaces are physically UP, the IP addresses are
correctly in the same /24, MTU is 1500 on both sides, Hello/Dead timers
match (10s/40s), and the network type is BROADCAST on both — there is
nothing physically or timer-wise wrong. The sole fault is the area ID
carried in OSPF Hello packets: when FRR receives a Hello whose area field
doesn't match the locally configured area, it silently discards it. As a
result, neither router ever advances the neighbor state beyond Init, and
both show "Neighbor Count is 0, Adjacent neighbor count is 0" on that
interface. router8's neighbor table has no entry for router7 at all, and
router7's neighbor table has no entry for 172.25.1.2, confirming the
adjacency never forms.

Root cause: router8's `network 172.25.1.0/24` statement was changed to
`area 7` instead of the correct `area 0`.

Corrective fix: on router8, enter vtysh and execute:
  router ospf
  no network 172.25.1.0/24 area 7
  network 172.25.1.0/24 area 0
This will re-align the area ID with router7, allow Hellos to be accepted,
and restore the Full adjacency. Campus 2 route flapping will cease once the
adjacency converges.
=== END ===
